Z||000000||1FieroGTRacingInc||03-24-2000||12:41 AM||||Tonight my 87GT turned over 99,000 miles... and NOW I REALLY SEE that 100,000 miles is next ...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>Is there ANYMORE SAD milestones that our beloved sportscar dreads????  ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Ed||03-24-2000||12:49 AM||||Cars are meant to be driven.  I've got 109,000 on my '87 GT and its going great.  Got all the perishables (rubber, etc.) replaced and just had it out for a 700 mile drive.  <P>Remember, these cars are/were critized for having Chevette and Citation parts.  The good news is, there are lots of Chevette and Citation parts.  Have you looked for Borgward parts lately?  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||kevin||03-24-2000||02:34 AM||||I used to feel that I should "save" my car by not accumalating miles. After pondering this thought, I decided I was a knucklehead. I thought of all the time spent late a night deciding what ideas would be uniquely incorporated to make my beauty run better, faster, stronger. How much time did I set aside reviewing new ideas on the Forum? What can work, what would be foolish? After awhile you get engrossed all day thinking how much money to be put aside for next months project. To simply park your dream in the garage has a much sense as adding a screen door to a submarine. How would you like to be 60+ years old and tell your freinds that you had(or still own), a Fiero but only drive it 400 miles a year for the past 25 years. I would rather brag I drove it 300,000 miles a brag about all the numerous fantasies and giggles along the way. After all, a number on the odometer is jsut that, a number. Do not stress-- DRIVE!!     ||||reg||1
